§11  the read path — what actually reaches these coordinates
  ok    requirement() READS in_scope, once per line, BEFORE anything else   [13, 33] -- so `in_scope` is not unread: `quality/revise.py`'s `grade()` and `inspect()` both call `requirement()` per pair
  ok    and UNDECLARED is reachable through NOTHING ELSE — scope is its only source   -- which is why a mandate with no scope can never return it, and every production mandate has no scope

    a scope that leaves out a line the mandate's OWN groups name is a contradiction, and it used to be built silently
  ok    a scope excluding a MANDATED line REFUSES   -- pairs() mandated L1/L3 while requirement(1,3) called it UNDECLARED, and grade() reads BOTH
  ok    a scope excluding a RETURNED line REFUSES too
  ok    re-opening a mandate to ADD a scope is checked the same way   -- that path took `scope` on trust, not even the 1..n bound
  ok    and the honest scope — one that covers every line the mandate names — is unaffected

    repeat_is_violation — the METHOD is documented, the FIELD is what the grader reads, and nothing pinned them together
  ok    Mandate.repeat_is_violation(i,j) IS requirement(i,j).repeat_is_violation at every pair of a real villanelle   171 pairs -- `quality/revise.py` calls `requirement(i, j).decided('repeat_is_violation')` and never the method, so changing the method alone would leave §2/§9 green and change nothing about a real grading run
  ok    and at every pair of a plain letter scheme with no returns

    the exact (is_declared, value) tuple `grade()` consumes
  ok    REQUIRE_RETURN -> (True, False): the refrain is REQUIRED to repeat, at all 12 villanelle return pairs   -- grade() skips these, which is why a correct villanelle scores 0 violations
  ok    REQUIRE_RHYME -> (True, True): an ordinary repeat in the same rhyme class is still charged   -- L1 and L4 are both class 'a' and are NOT the same line
  ok    FREE and UNDECLARED -> (False, None): these two, and only these two, fall through to the song-wide repeat_licence switch

    FINDING — the fallback is NOT what its own comment says, and the difference is measurable here
  ok    a plain letter scheme is NOT 'UNDECLARED for this question on every pair': its MANDATED pairs are declared REQUIRE_RHYME   -- `quality/revise.py` grade() L480-483 claims otherwise. Because `declared` is True there, `is_violation if declared else not default_licensed` never reads the switch, so repeat_licence='refrain' is INERT on every letter scheme and Cover-without-returns. Only the DEFAULT 'unlicensed' reproduces the pre-fix behaviour
  ok    a letter scheme cannot state the question at all, which is why REQUIRE_RHYME's True is this module's DEFAULT and not the writer's declaration   -- doctrine 3 inverts BY CONTEXT and a letter has two states for a question with five answers; the fix belongs at the call site, not in this value

§12  _rep — which member of an identity class the licence transports FROM
  ok    a returned line's representative is the FIRST member of its class   -- L33 IS L13, so every question about L33 is a question about L13

    THE ANSWER THAT MOVES, on the chorus whose rhyme is declared ONCE and TRANSPORTED (doctrine 3: REPEAT inverts by context)
  ok    L17/L33 is REQUIRE_RHYME — L33 IS L13, L13/L17 must rhyme, so an identical end word at L17/L33 is a VIOLATION   -- the exact tuple grade() consumes
  ok    and read from the LAST member the SAME pair answers something ELSE, so this input DISCRIMINATES   -- L17 would represent as L37 and L33 as L33, no declared group holds that pair, and the violation is LICENSED AWAY in silence
  ok    the returned chorus's OWN internal rhyme moves the same way   -- L33/L37 IS L13/L17 and nothing else in the mandate says so
  ok    12 of this mandate's 820 pairs answer differently under the two readings, and all 12 touch the transported chorus   [(13, 37), (14, 36), (15, 39), (16, 34), (17, 33), (18, 40), (19, 35), (20, 38), (33, 37), (34, 36), (35, 39), (38, 40)]

